      "text": "Let the common difference of the arithmetic progression be $d$.\nThen, we have the equations:\n\n$10 + d = a$\n$a + d = b$\n$b + d = ab$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have $a = 10 + d$.\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get:\n\n$(10 + d) + d = b \\Rightarrow b = 10 + 2d$\n\nSubstituting $a = 10 + d$ and $b = 10 + 2d$ into the third equation, we get:\n\n$(10 + 2d) + d = (10 + d)(10 + 2d) \\Rightarrow 10 + 3d = 100 + 30d + 2d^2$\n\nRearranging and simplifying, we have:\n\n$2d^2 - 27d + 90 = 0$\n\nFactoring, we get:\n\n$(2d - 10)(d - 9) = 0$\n\nSo, $d = 5$ or $d = 9$.\n\nFor $d = 5$, we have $a = 10 + 5 = 15$, $b = 10 + 2(5) = 20$, and $ab = (10 + 5)(10 + 2(5)) = 225$.\nFor $d = 9$, we have $a = 10 + 9 = 19$, $b = 10 + 2(9) = 28$, and $ab = (10 + 9)(10 + 2(9)) = 360$.\n\nThus, there are $\\boxed{2}$ pairs $(a,b)$ that satisfy the given conditions.\nThe answer is: 2",
