      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression, we need to use the property of arithmetic progressions. In an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let this common difference be \\(d\\).\n\nThe terms of the arithmetic progression are:\n1. \\(10\\)\n2. \\(a\\)\n3. \\(b\\)\n4. \\(ab\\)\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) can be expressed in two different ways:\n1. \\(a - 10 = d\\)\n2. \\(b - a = d\\)\n3. \\(ab - b = d\\)\n\nFrom the first two expressions, we have:\n\\[d = a - 10\\]\n\\[d = b - a\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(d\\), we get:\n\\[a - 10 = b - a\\]\n\\[2a = b + 10\\]\n\\[b = 2a - 10\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into the third expression \\(d = ab - b\\), we get:\n\\[d = a(2a - 10) - (2a - 10)\\]\n\\[d = 2a^2 - 10a - 2a + 10\\]\n\\[d = 2a^2 - 12a + 10\\]\n\nSince \\(d = a - 10\\), we can set the two expressions for \\(d\\) equal to each other:\n\\[a - 10 = 2a^2 - 12a + 10\\]\n\\[2a^2 - 13a + 20 = 0\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -13\\), and \\(c = 20\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{(-13)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 20}}{2 \\cdot 2}\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{169 - 160}}{4}\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{9}}{4}\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm 3}{4}\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(a\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{16}{4} = 4\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2}\\]\n\nFor each value of \\(a\\), we find the corresponding value of \\(b\\):\n1. If \\(a = 4\\), then \\(b = 2(4) - 10 = 8 - 10 = -2\\). So, the pair is \\((4, -2)\\).\n2. If \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\), then \\(b = 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 = 5 - 10 = -5\\). So, the pair is \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\).\n\nThus, there are exactly two pairs \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy the condition. The final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]",
