      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression, we need to use the property of arithmetic progressions. In an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Therefore, the difference between \\(a\\) and \\(10\\) is the same as the difference between \\(b\\) and \\(a\\), and this difference is also the same as the difference between \\(ab\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nThis gives us the following system of equations:\n1. \\(a - 10 = b - a\\)\n2. \\(b - a = ab - b\\)\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\\[a - 10 = b - a\\]\n\\[2a = b + 10\\]\n\\[b = 2a - 10\\]\n\nFrom the second equation:\n\\[b - a = ab - b\\]\n\\[2b - a = ab\\]\nSubstitute \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into the equation:\n\\[2(2a - 10) - a = a(2a - 10)\\]\n\\[4a - 20 - a = 2a^2 - 10a\\]\n\\[3a - 20 = 2a^2 - 10a\\]\n\\[2a^2 - 13a + 20 = 0\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -13\\), and \\(c = 20\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{169 - 160}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{9}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm 3}{4}\\]\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(a\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{16}{4} = 4\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2}\\]\n\nNow, we find the corresponding values of \\(b\\) using \\(b = 2a - 10\\):\n1. If \\(a = 4\\), then \\(b = 2(4) - 10 = 8 - 10 = -2\\).\n2. If \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\), then \\(b = 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 = 5 - 10 = -5\\).\n\nTherefore, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are \\((4, -2)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\).\n\nThe number of such pairs is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
